\section{Applications and connections}
\textbf{Spectral graph theory.} Boolean degree one functions are the $\{0,1\}$-vectors
in $V_0\oplus V_1$ of the distance-regular Grassmann graph. \textbf{Coding theory and
designs.} Cameron--Liebler classes correspond to tight sets and yield two-weight codes
and designs. \textbf{Field arithmetic as obstruction.} Parity of $q$ and the $q=3$
threshold show how number theory governs geometric existence. \textbf{$q$-Krawtchouk
bridge.} In $H(n,2)$, degree-one Boolean functions are governed by $K_1(x;n)=n-2x$; the
Grassmann scheme is the $q$-analogue, with the midpoint $x=(q^2+1)/2$ the balance point
of the $q$-linear functional.

\section{Discussion: separating arithmetic from geometry}
We factor the Bruen--Drudge result into an unconditional arithmetic core (Sections
2--3) and a hypothesis-driven deduction (Section 4). The only geometric input is the
inhabitation of \texttt{CLClass} at the midpoint; everything else is number theory plus
the degree-one counting identity. A class at parameter $x$ is non-trivial iff
$2<x<q^2-1$ and non-constant iff $0<x<q^2+1$.

\section{Future directions}
\begin{itemize}
\item[C1.] The non-trivial window is non-empty iff $q\ge 3$; prove the $q=2$
impossibility as a finite enumeration on the $35$-line geometry.
\item[C2.] Every self-complementary Boolean degree one function forces $q$ odd and
support size $(q^2+1)(q^2+q+1)/2$.
\item[C3.] Parameter rigidity: $\{0,1,2,q^2-1,q^2,q^2+1\}$ is the unique maximal subset
of $\{0,\dots,q^2+1\}$ closed under complementation and realized for all $q$.
\item[C4.] A $q$-Krawtchouk eigenvalue bridge to the Hamming scheme, identifying
Boolean degree one functions as $\{0,1\}$-eigenvectors of the first $q$-Krawtchouk
eigenvalue.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}
The midpoint parameter $\bdParam(q)=\lfloor(q^2+1)/2\rfloor$ is a genuine
self-complementary integer exactly for odd $q$, and lies strictly inside the
non-trivial window $(2,q^2-1)$ for all $q\ge 3$. With a minimal model of a
Cameron--Liebler class and the line counts $q^2+q+1$ and $(q^2+1)(q^2+q+1)$, any class
realized at the midpoint (for $q\ge 3$) is a non-constant --- hence non-trivial ---
Boolean degree one function on $J_q(4,2)$, cleanly separating the unconditional number
theory from the geometric realizability of the Bruen--Drudge theorem.

